Chameleon: optionally don't announce the XWAYLAND extension.

Mesa's Vulkan WSI checks for this extension. When it finds it, it presents
without waiting for the GPU and, in mailbox mode, asynchronously. When it
does not, it behaves as it does with Termux:X11: in mailbox mode it waits for
each frame's fence before presenting it. With CHAMELEON_XWAYLAND_PLAIN_X=1 in
Xwayland's environment the extension is not registered, so stock Mesa gives
this server the same treatment.

This is an experiment: other programs that look for Xwayland (to work around
its pointer or window quirks) will also stop seeing it.

Written against xserver master of 2024-05-09 (just before xwayland 24.1.0).

--- a/hw/xwayland/xwayland.c
+++ b/hw/xwayland/xwayland.c
@@ -413,6 +413,18 @@
 static void
 xwlExtensionInit(void)
 {
+    /* Chameleon: clients find out that this is Xwayland from this extension.
+     * Mesa's Vulkan WSI then presents without waiting for the GPU, because a
+     * compositor normally waits on the buffer's fence. On Android nothing
+     * attaches one, so the frame may be read half-drawn. Without the
+     * extension Mesa treats this server as it does any other X server, and
+     * in mailbox mode waits for each frame itself before presenting it.
+     */
+    const char *plain = getenv("CHAMELEON_XWAYLAND_PLAIN_X");
+
+    if (plain && strcmp(plain, "1") == 0)
+        return;
+
     AddExtension(XWAYLAND_EXTENSION_NAME,
                  XwlNumberEvents, XwlNumberErrors,
                  ProcXwaylandDispatch, SProcXwaylandDispatch,
